ANALGESICS
A Practical Pharmaceutical & Clinical Reference Guide

For pharmacy students, pharmacists, nurses and healthcare learners
Prepared as an educational reference • Adult dosing emphasized
2026 EDITION

IMPORTANT: This guide is educational and is not a substitute for an individual prescription, current product label, national treatment guideline, or pharmacist/doctor assessment. Doses vary with indication, age, weight, renal/hepatic function, pregnancy, formulation and local product strength.
1. Executive overview
Analgesics are medicines used to relieve pain. They include non-opioid analgesics such as paracetamol, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), and opioid analgesics. Choice should be based on pain severity, cause, inflammation, patient factors, contraindications, interactions and duration of therapy.
•	Paracetamol (acetaminophen): mainly analgesic and antipyretic; little anti-inflammatory activity at usual doses.
•	NSAIDs: provide analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory effects by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) pathways.
•	Opioids: act mainly at opioid receptors and are reserved for selected moderate-to-severe pain because of sedation, respiratory depression, dependence and overdose risks.
•	Topical/local analgesics can be useful when pain is localized and systemic exposure should be minimized.
2. Major classes of analgesics
Class	Examples	Main role	Major cautions
Non-opioid analgesic	Paracetamol	Mild–moderate pain; fever	Liver toxicity in overdose; duplicate ingredients
NSAIDs	Ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac, aspirin	Pain with inflammation; musculoskeletal pain	GI bleeding/ulcer, renal injury, fluid retention, cardiovascular risk
Opioids	Morphine, tramadol, codeine	Moderate–severe selected pain	Respiratory depression, sedation, constipation, dependence; interactions
Topical/local	Diclofenac gel, lidocaine preparations	Localized pain; selected neuropathic/local pain	Skin reactions; formulation-specific restrictions
3. Common analgesics: practical reference
Medicine	Typical adult dose*	Administration	Prescription / access note	Storage
Paracetamol 500 mg	500–1,000 mg every 4–6 h; follow product maximum. Many adult labels use max 4,000 mg/day, while routine self-care may use lower limits.	Oral; some products rectal/IV under clinical supervision	Often OTC; some strengths/forms may require prescription	Usually room/ambient temperature; follow label
Ibuprofen	200–400 mg every 4–6 h for common OTC use; prescription regimens may differ	Oral; take with/after food if stomach upset	Often OTC at low strengths; higher doses may be prescription	Room/ambient temperature; protect from heat/moisture
Naproxen	Dose depends strongly on indication/formulation; commonly 250–500 mg per dose in adults under prescribed regimens	Oral; with/after food	Often prescription; some products/pharmacies may have non-prescription access	Room/ambient temperature; follow label
Diclofenac	Dose depends on formulation and indication; commonly 25–50 mg 2–3 times daily for selected adult indications, with product-specific maximum	Oral/topical/rectal depending product	Usually prescription in many settings; topical products may have different access	Follow product label; protect from excessive heat
Aspirin for pain	300–900 mg per dose at intervals of at least 4 h; follow product maximum	Oral; preferably with/after food	Pain use may be pharmacy/OTC; low-dose antiplatelet use should be clinician-directed	Dry place at label temperature; protect from moisture
Morphine	Highly indication- and route-dependent; prescribed/titrated individually	Oral, SC, IM, IV and other hospital routes	Controlled/prescription medicine	Secure controlled-drug storage; follow institutional requirements
Tramadol	Typically 50–100 mg every 4–6 h when prescribed; maximum depends on formulation and patient factors	Oral; immediate/extended-release forms differ	Prescription/controlled status varies by jurisdiction	Secure and follow product storage instructions
Codeine	Dose depends on combination/product and indication; prescription required in many settings	Oral	Prescription/regulated in many settings	Secure where required; follow label
*Doses are educational adult examples, not individualized prescriptions. Always verify the exact formulation, indication, local guideline and patient-specific factors before administration.
4. Paracetamol (acetaminophen)
Uses include headache, dental pain, musculoskeletal pain, fever and other mild-to-moderate pain. It is commonly used when an NSAID is unsuitable.
•	Adult 500 mg tablets: commonly 1–2 tablets per dose, at least 4 hours apart; NHS guidance states no more than 8 × 500 mg tablets in 24 hours for standard adult use.
•	A 1 g formulation is normally one tablet per dose, with no more than four 1 g tablets in 24 hours according to the cited adult guidance.
•	Do not combine multiple paracetamol-containing products without checking total daily exposure. Combination cold/flu products frequently contain paracetamol.
•	Liver disease, low body weight, heavy alcohol use, malnutrition and interacting medicines can require a lower dose or professional assessment.
IMPORTANT: Overdose can cause severe liver injury and may be fatal. FDA states that total acetaminophen from all sources should not exceed 4,000 mg/day in adults and people aged 12 years and older; product-specific labels may set lower limits.
5. NSAIDs: ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac and aspirin
NSAIDs reduce prostaglandin synthesis and therefore reduce pain and inflammation. They can be effective for dental, musculoskeletal, menstrual and inflammatory pain, but their risk profile differs from paracetamol.
•	Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest appropriate duration.
•	Avoid taking two systemic NSAIDs together, such as ibuprofen plus naproxen, unless specifically directed by a qualified clinician.
•	Important risks include dyspepsia, gastritis, peptic ulceration, GI bleeding, renal impairment, fluid retention and cardiovascular events.
•	Use extra caution in older adults and in patients with kidney disease, heart failure, hypertension, previous ulcer/GI bleeding or anticoagulant use.
•	NSAIDs can interact with anticoagulants, corticosteroids, some antidepressants, antihypertensives and diuretics.
•	Pregnancy requires professional review; NSAIDs are generally avoided in pregnancy unless specifically advised.
6. Aspirin: pain relief versus antiplatelet therapy
Aspirin has two very different common uses. Higher doses may relieve pain, while low doses are used for antiplatelet therapy in selected patients.
•	For pain, NHS adult guidance describes 300 mg tablets, 1–3 tablets per dose, at least 4 hours apart, with no more than 4 doses/day.
•	Low-dose aspirin such as 75 mg once daily is not simply a stronger or weaker painkiller; it is commonly prescribed for antiplatelet indications.
•	Do not start or stop low-dose aspirin for cardiovascular prevention without clinical advice.
•	Do not give aspirin to children under 16 unless specifically prescribed.
7. Opioid analgesics
Opioids are reserved for carefully selected moderate-to-severe pain. Dosing must be individualized and depends on opioid, route, formulation, opioid tolerance, age, renal/hepatic function and indication.
Medicine	Key point	Major risks / precautions
Morphine	Strong opioid; dose titrated individually, especially in acute/hospital pain	Respiratory depression, sedation, hypotension, nausea, constipation; renal impairment affects metabolite handling
Tramadol	Opioid with additional monoaminergic activity; formulation-specific dosing	Seizures, serotonin syndrome with serotonergic medicines, sedation, respiratory depression, dependence
Codeine	Often used in combination products; response varies because metabolism to morphine differs between people	Sedation, constipation, respiratory depression; variable efficacy; avoid in children in many settings
Oxycodone	Potent opioid used for selected moderate-to-severe pain	Respiratory depression, constipation, dependence, overdose; requires careful prescribing
IMPORTANT: Never provide a generic opioid dose as though it were a universal prescription. Opioid therapy requires patient-specific assessment, monitoring, interaction review and secure storage/controlled-drug procedures.
8. Administration routes and correct technique
Route	Examples	Key administration points
Oral	Paracetamol, ibuprofen, naproxen, tramadol	Swallow with appropriate fluid; follow food instructions; do not crush modified-release/gastro-resistant products
Topical	Diclofenac gel, lidocaine preparations	Apply only to intact skin according to product instructions; wash hands; avoid eyes/mucosa unless formulation permits
Rectal	Some paracetamol/diclofenac products	Use only the intended suppository formulation; follow hygiene and insertion instructions
Intravenous	Paracetamol, morphine and others in clinical settings	Administer only by trained personnel using the correct dilution, rate and monitoring requirements
IM/SC	Selected opioids and other agents	Clinical administration; route is product-specific and not interchangeable
Buccal/transmucosal	Selected opioid formulations	Do not substitute with ordinary oral tablets; follow formulation-specific instructions
9. Prescription and dispensing principles
•	A prescription should identify the patient, medicine, strength, dosage form, dose, route, frequency, duration/quantity and prescriber details as required by local law.
•	Before dispensing, check allergies, age, pregnancy status where relevant, renal/hepatic function, current medicines and duplicate analgesic ingredients.
•	Check whether the medicine is OTC, pharmacy-only, prescription-only or controlled under the applicable Ugandan regulatory framework.
•	For opioids and other controlled medicines, comply with applicable controlled-drug record, storage and dispensing requirements.
•	Counsel the patient on what the medicine treats, how to take it, maximum dose, duration, important interactions and danger signs.
•	Do not assume that a medicine sold without a prescription is risk-free.
10. Expiry dates and beyond-use dates
The expiry date (EXP) is the manufacturer's established limit for the unopened product under specified storage conditions. A beyond-use date may apply after a product is opened, reconstituted, compounded or otherwise prepared, depending on the product and pharmacy procedure.
•	Never dispense or use a medicine after its labeled expiry date.
•	Do not guess an expiry date from the manufacturing date unless the manufacturer explicitly provides the required calculation.
•	For products with only month/year expiry, follow the manufacturer's labeling convention and local policy.
•	Reconstituted liquids may have a shorter in-use period than the unopened product. Write the date of reconstitution/opening when appropriate.
•	Do not use tablets, liquids or creams whose physical appearance suggests deterioration, even if the printed expiry date has not passed; seek pharmacist/manufacturer advice.
IMPORTANT: Expiry is not a universal number of years. It must be read from the individual package. This guide therefore does not invent expiry dates for brands.
11. Storage of analgesics
Storage principle	Recommended practice
Temperature	Follow the package/leaflet. Many ambient medicines are kept around 15–25°C in controlled pharmacy storage; manufacturer limits take precedence.
Moisture	Keep tightly closed and away from bathrooms, sinks and humid areas.
Heat	Do not leave medicines in direct sunlight, vehicles or near heat sources.
Light	Protect light-sensitive products when the label requires it; keep in original packaging when appropriate.
Children	Store out of sight and reach of children.
Controlled medicines	Store securely according to national law and institutional controlled-drug procedures.
Liquid medicines	Keep tightly closed; use the supplied measuring device; observe refrigeration or post-opening instructions.
Packaging	Keep original packaging where possible so name, strength, batch number and expiry remain identifiable.
12. Important contraindications / caution checklist
Patient factor	Analgesic concern
Liver disease / heavy alcohol use	Paracetamol requires careful dose assessment; overdose risk is particularly important.
Peptic ulcer / GI bleeding	Avoid or carefully assess NSAIDs; consider safer alternatives where appropriate.
Kidney disease / dehydration	NSAIDs can worsen renal function; professional assessment required.
Heart failure / significant cardiovascular disease	NSAIDs may cause fluid retention and increase cardiovascular risk.
Anticoagulant therapy	NSAIDs/aspirin can increase bleeding risk; check before use.
Pregnancy	Choice depends on trimester and indication; obtain professional advice, especially for NSAIDs.
Asthma with previous NSAID reaction	Avoid implicated NSAIDs and seek alternatives.
Child / adolescent	Dose is often weight-based and product-specific; aspirin is generally avoided under 16 unless prescribed.
Older adult	Higher risk of renal, GI, CNS and interaction-related adverse effects; start cautiously when appropriate.
Substance-use disorder / opioid risk	Opioid prescribing requires careful risk assessment, monitoring and safe storage.
13. Common drug interactions and duplication errors
•	Paracetamol + paracetamol-containing cold/flu product → accidental overdose.
•	Ibuprofen + naproxen/aspirin → unnecessary NSAID duplication and increased adverse effects.
•	NSAID + anticoagulant → increased bleeding risk.
•	NSAID + corticosteroid → increased GI injury/bleeding risk.
•	Tramadol + serotonergic antidepressants → serotonin syndrome risk.
•	Tramadol/opioid + alcohol or sedatives → increased CNS and respiratory depression.
•	Always check active ingredients, not only brand names.
14. Patient counselling: a pharmacist's checklist
•	Confirm the patient's identity, pain complaint, severity, duration and any red-flag symptoms.
•	Confirm allergies and previous reactions to analgesics.
•	Ask what medicines, supplements and traditional/herbal products the patient is using.
•	Explain the exact dose, interval, route and maximum daily amount.
•	Explain whether the medicine should be taken with food.
•	Warn against duplicate active ingredients and taking multiple NSAIDs together.
•	Explain important adverse effects and symptoms requiring urgent help.
•	Explain safe storage and keeping medicines away from children.
•	Ask the patient to repeat the instructions if there is any doubt about correct use.
15. Red flags requiring medical assessment
•	Severe or sudden unexplained pain, chest pain, severe abdominal pain or severe headache.
•	Pain following major trauma or suspected fracture.
•	Persistent fever, worsening pain, or pain that does not improve as expected.
•	Vomiting blood, black/tarry stools or other evidence of GI bleeding.
•	Difficulty breathing, facial swelling, severe rash or suspected anaphylaxis.
•	Marked drowsiness, slow/shallow breathing or inability to wake after an opioid.
•	Suspected overdose, particularly with paracetamol or opioids.
•	New neurological symptoms, severe weakness or confusion.
16. Quick comparison for pharmacy practice
Feature	Paracetamol	NSAIDs	Opioids
Pain relief	Yes	Yes	Yes; often stronger
Inflammation	Limited at usual doses	Yes	No direct anti-inflammatory effect
Fever	Yes	Yes	Not preferred
GI ulcer/bleeding	Less typical	Important risk	Not the main risk
Kidney risk	Generally lower at therapeutic doses	Important risk	Dose/selection issues in renal impairment
Liver toxicity	Major overdose concern	Possible but less characteristic	Possible depending on agent
Respiratory depression	No	No	Major risk
Dependence potential	No	No typical dependence syndrome	Yes
Typical self-care role	Common	Common for selected patients	Generally clinician-directed
17. Practical dispensing examples
Example A — uncomplicated adult headache:
•	Assess for red flags and medication duplication.
•	Paracetamol may be considered if appropriate, following the product label and patient's risk factors.
•	If an NSAID is considered, screen for ulcer/GI bleeding, kidney disease, anticoagulants, pregnancy and other NSAIDs.
Example B — musculoskeletal pain with inflammation:
•	An NSAID may be appropriate for a suitable patient for the shortest effective period.
•	Topical diclofenac may be considered for localized pain when the formulation is appropriate.
Example C — severe acute pain:
•	Do not simply increase OTC analgesic doses. Assess the cause and severity and refer for clinical management; an opioid may be considered only when appropriately prescribed and monitored.
18. Key safety rules — remember these
•	Never exceed the labeled or prescribed dose.
•	Never assume two different brand names contain different active ingredients.
•	Do not combine systemic NSAIDs unless specifically instructed.
•	Keep opioids secure and never share them.
•	Check expiry and storage conditions before dispensing or administration.
•	Use the exact measuring device supplied for liquid medicines.
•	Ask a pharmacist/doctor when pregnancy, chronic disease, multiple medicines, severe pain or overdose is involved.
•	Dispose of expired/unwanted medicines through an appropriate pharmacy or medicine-waste pathway where available.
